J71b Apple-derived power reference (unwired kernel adapter)
================================================================

This is the first separately implemented core for the requested second power
approach. It starts from upstream df7c930, independently of the existing
j71b/power-validation driver. It is NOT a second bootable image or a completed
Apple-equivalent Linux driver. The kernel-compatible core and default-off
POWER_RESET_APPLE_D2365_REFERENCE adapter now exist, but no platform driver,
DT node or board calls the registration API. It is built-in only because
ARM64's terminal machine_restart entry point is not exported to modules.
Compilation alone registers
nothing. Do not wire it to hardware as-is.

The registration API requires J71b, a probing consumer, an 8-bit regmap, a
same-PMIC apple,d2365-pmic-gpio event owner, an explicit source snapshot
provider and a terminal runtime-option provider. It pins bound provider
modules/devices while creating managed links and rolls back handlers/pins on failure. A failed caller probe lets driver
core clean managed links; callbacks must belong to the pinned provider modules.
Providers must be fully bound, not already entering teardown even if their
driver remains attached. This is checked while holding each provider's lock.
Lock acquisition is nonblocking: a locked provider causes probe deferral,
including a parent that synchronously populates children from its own probe.
The caller must still have a reprobe path: arbitrary lock release alone does
not retrigger deferred probing, whereas a parent's successful probe does.
The PMIC transport must still be operational after device_shutdown(), and its
regmap must bypass caching for events, status and commands and never be in
cache-only mode during callbacks. These transport properties are caller
contracts, not properties established by device links or the 8-bit check.
The adapter checks existing poweroff handlers and uses unique HIGH priority.
This does NOT guarantee global exclusivity: board integration must exclude both
the existing driver and later competing handlers.

The adapter now uses the real D2365 GPIO provider, not a caller-supplied event
callback. It checks the bound driver and event domain after taking the managed
supplier dependency, then uses that owner's quiesce API. The API closes event,
retry and mask-update gates, holds and drains the parent IRQ, and cancels retry
work before returning ownership of the read-to-clear bank. It is a terminal
shutdown handoff, not a reversible suspend operation. No new GPIO shutdown
callback or all-board behavior change is added. The adapter requires this GPIO
driver built-in, so there is no built-in-to-module symbol dependency.

Invalid ownership prevents all core PMIC operations. Normal restart leaves reset to
the existing platform driver. Poweroff remains unresolved and holds instead.
Source sampling and bus transactions run only in sleepable prepare; final
handling resets or waits without further PMIC access. The final wait spins with
IRQs disabled, unlike Apple's sleep loop: it is a Linux terminal adaptation,
not proof of electrical off or a justified power-consumption policy. The
adapter's unresolved-state hold is containment, not identical Apple behavior.

Run ./run.sh, python3 test_adapter.py and python3 test_event_provider.py.
Requires an existing C compiler and pthreads. CFLAGS may enable sanitizers.
The runner uses a temporary executable and removes it on exit. No firmware
bytes are distributed or required to run tests.

Distinct behavior implemented
-----------------------------
* Separate logical Apple raw-external and USB-cable properties. No 0x018a
  substitute, charger ONLINE shortcut, or repeated source rechecks.
* Ordinary sequence: read events, policy mask, RTC/no-alarm, clear boot stage
  and flags, sample properties, preserve boot-state bits, then either return
  toward platform reset or prepare GPIOs and request standby.
* Fresh paired GPIO bytes before writing. GPIO helper failure is recorded
  and the enclosing standby sequence continues, unlike the existing Linux
  driver's abort-to-reset policy.
* Write/event errors are recorded without automatically selecting reset.
* Terminal actions are values: PLATFORM_RESET and STANDBY_WAIT do not perform
  a reset or an indefinite wait. A standby write error still leads to Apple's
  wait terminal, but its transaction outcome is explicitly unacknowledged.
* Ordinary restart attempts event acknowledgement and boot-stage clear even
  when the first operation fails, then returns the platform-reset terminal.

Limits and deliberately non-identical cases
------------------------------------------
No hardware source-property producer is implemented. ABSENT means an observed
absent Apple property/object, not a missing Linux provider. A successful coherent
snapshot with either independently valid TRUE determines the reset branch even
if the other property is UNRESOLVED; it does not invent that property's value.
Standby requires both properties resolved. Callback errors invalidate the whole
snapshot, and malformed values are rejected even alongside TRUE. UNRESOLVED
must not be mapped to ABSENT or FALSE to make a device boot or shut down.
Source resolution happens at the corresponding point in the modeled sequence;
so it can return unresolved after earlier simulated writes. Hardware integration
must validate the entire producer contract before registering any callback.

The core's normal_path_established input only expresses a test precondition.
The adapter now requires prepare_runtime on a separately pinned/linked provider
and checks its result in sleepable poweroff preparation after device_shutdown,
before taking event ownership or invoking any core PMIC operation. Every RTC
alarm, UPO delay, charge-trap, external-shutdown and diagnostic option must be
explicitly DISABLED. Zero/unknown, enabled, malformed or omitted fields reject
the ordinary path; callback errors invalidate all output. The callback must
drain option producers and exclude later changes until reset/off, not merely
return a cached probe-time snapshot. It must not consume the GPIO owner's
read-to-clear bank. This is an enforced interface contract, not an implemented
runtime provider or proof that the actual board options have been discovered.
Ordinary restart does not call the runtime-option provider. Scheduled RTC/UPO,
special shutdown mode, an external controller and diagnostic reads remain
unsupported; rejection leaves terminal handling in the unresolved hold.
Failed 0x500f/0x0400 reads return UNRESOLVED instead of reusing Apple's scratch
byte. This is an explicit modeling boundary, not a claim that Apple aborts.
No return value means electrical-off success. A successful I2C write would
not prove that either. GPIO and event errors may make continued standby unsafe
for a live Linux system; this model does not authorize that policy on hardware.

Before hardware activation
--------------------------
1. Establish actual J71b producers and semantics for BOTH Apple source
   properties, including absence, stale state, teardown, and cable transitions.
   Current Linux Tristar ONLINE is only cached 0x018a != 0, not a mapped source.
2. Implement and verify the terminal runtime-option provider, including actual
   option ownership and frozen lifetime after device_shutdown. Typed admission
   checks reject unsupported/unknown modes but cannot discover them themselves.
3. Verify the wired provider/IRQ/I2C lifetimes during Linux shutdown. The
   concrete event API implements a handoff, but has not been hardware-qualified.
4. Decide and document live read-error policy and nonreturning terminal handling.
   Do not silently import a one-second wait or fail-to-reset behavior and call
   that Apple-equivalent. Do not blindly copy unchecked Apple reads either.
5. Review and compile each integrated revision against the real kernel,
   add providers/bindings only once their contracts are grounded, then run
   kernel integration tests and request a bounded device test.

Additional source-contract evidence
-----------------------------------
The D2365 source dictionary's 0x189 bit-1 requirement is state-dependent:
the charger passes inverse previous source status to the backend getter
(0x5f161e4-0x5f16204; D2365 selection at 0x6b85294). It is not a fixed mask.
The immediate charger USB override is function-usb_det, not the separate
function-usb_dmonitor facility (0x5f15b30-0x5f15b9c, 0x5f163b4-0x5f163e4).
AppleUSBCableDetect is published from a cached IOAccessoryUSBPower-derived
value (0x5ed36c0-0x5ed37fc), not a fresh shutdown-time PMIC read. Source
selection, all update paths and teardown/staleness guarantees remain unproven.

Ordinary-mode inputs also require runtime evidence: upo-shutdown-delay
(0x6b7e514-0x6b7e5a8), pmu-chargetrap or resolved software-behavior bit 6
(0x6b7e744-0x6b7e7ec), function-external_shutdown (0x6b7e8cc-0x6b7e8e8),
mutable RTC alarm state (0x6b87118-0x6b87164) and debug configuration
(0x6b7c030-0x6b7c0bc). The supplied firmware DeviceTree's software-behavior
is a syscfg substitution descriptor, not resolved runtime bytes. It cannot
establish the ordinary-path precondition just because the board is J71b.
These facts narrow the missing contracts; they do not implement a provider.

Further tracing resolves the ordinary USB distinction: raw-external state is
computed from reduced source info and requires resolved current greater than
100, then passes classification/backend-load checks (0x5f16ea0-0x5f17618).
Default source info without adapter/current events resolves current to zero.
Cable detection instead follows the selected source's effective status, with
pending changes held until the maximum contributed debounce-event deadline
(0x5efc3d0, 0x5efc54c, 0x5efbda8). Thus cable=true and raw=false is valid.
The missing implementation must supply source selection, current/adapter events,
deadline ordering and startup validity, not merely another status-register read.

Inspected Linux transport and ownership
--------------------------------------
J71b's PMIC is the simple-mfd-i2c apple,i2c-pmic device with a 16-bit-address,
8-bit-value REGCACHE_NONE regmap. Its MFD has no shutdown/PM method that
disables or caches the bus. The Apple PASemi platform controller retains its
devm-enabled reference clock and IRQ and has no shutdown/PM callback. The PMGR
power-state driver has no shutdown callback; its ordinary runtime/remove
paths still matter. These source facts support this specific prepare path,
not every future runtime/unbind configuration.

Both prepare chains execute after device_shutdown and before syscore_shutdown.
The PMIC I2C client has no own IRQ; disabling its clients does not stop the GPIO
event thread. The concrete GPIO handoff is therefore required. Tristar shutdown
stops polling and disables nested charger IRQs while preserving cached online
state; that does not provide either missing Apple source contract. No MFD,
I2C, PMGR or charging-policy change is part of this integration.

Evidence provenance (facts/algorithm, no proprietary code)
---------------------------------------------------------
Exact J71b/iPad7,5 T8010 kernel, iPadOS build 21H461; decompressed kernel SHA256:
5c0822a78a10191b56edd468f100b331b0e86bcafa3fdcc5d974dadc427dbec9

Addresses below are unslid ARM64 virtual addresses with prefix fffffff0.
006b826c0-006b82774: initial events, 30-byte policy mask, no-alarm RTC and clears.
006b827b4-006b8287c: AppleARMPMUPowerSource AppleRawExternalConnected and separate
IOService resource AppleUSBCableDetect, compared with true.
006b82890-006b828f4: boot state mask 0x27; ordinary non-standby flag 0x90.
006b8294c-006b82a68: GPIO helper, logged errors, event acknowledgement,
0x0400 read/bit0/write, then repeated wait.
006b8206c-006b82208: GPIO write helper refreshes 36 bytes and overlays even
configuration bytes before the bulk write and six separate writes.
006b7c794-006b7c7f0: shutdown caller proceeds to PE_halt_restart(1).
006b7b6e4: restart wrapper; 006b81048-006b81070: boot-stage property write.

Tests enumerate all 4x4 source property combinations and 256 boot-state bytes,
all GPIO configuration bytes with asynchronously refreshed pairs, each ordinary
sequence transaction failure (including partial and complete-before-error commitment), unsupported
mode rejection, and normal restart error continuation. They test our model,
not the original executable, kernel lifecycle, bus framing, or physical IRQs.

Adapter tests compile the actual C adapter against a host lifecycle shim.
They inject failure at every modeled allocation/link/action/handler operation,
check rollback of pins and handlers, reject unbound/unbinding/locked/missing
providers and wide-regmap inputs, check shared providers and both source reset
branches,
and check quiesce failures and bus-free terminal callbacks. The shim models
probe cleanup but cannot prove kernel unbind/shutdown races, PMGR/I2C lifetime,
or actual IRQ ownership. Kernel compilation and integration checks are separate.

test_event_provider.py compiles ten actual GPIO functions against explicit
host IRQ/work primitives. Deterministic threads pause both pre-read line and
post-read nested dispatch and verify the handoff waits for drain. Tests cover
queued/dequeued retries, already-held IRQ, repeated handoff, read-error retry,
readiness checks and no event read, mask write, requeue or reenable after return.
Quiesce leaves the IRQ allocated; later normal driver cleanup frees it once.
This tests the real function bodies and modeled boundaries, not full Linux IRQ
descriptor/workqueue semantics or electrical behavior.

Runtime-provider tests additionally enumerate all 4^5 combinations of five
options (unknown, disabled, enabled and malformed), plus negative enum values,
untouched output and callback errors. Changes after registration are sampled
at preparation; only five explicit DISABLED values pass. Rejected modes do not
take event ownership, sample sources, access the adapter regmap or reset in the
terminal callback. Runtime-provider pin/link rollback, unbinding/locked states,
shared providers, exact callback-device identity and restart bypass are checked.
The fixture does not prove a future provider can actually freeze RTC/option
producers: no such provider or board registration is implemented yet.

IDBus source-provider dependency
-------------------------------
The existing Tristar capability poll reads a cached eight-byte 0x75 response;
it neither submits Dock commands 0x70/0x72 nor receives their matched 0x71/0x73
responses. A successful cached capability read is not proof of USB route or
absence of accessory deadlines. The exact firmware has a serialized transport,
with one active job, expected RX length, timeout/cancel paths, CRC checks and
asynchronous owner callbacks. A valid 0x73 response populates cached four-byte
Dock state. A successful 0x71 in normal-handshake state 6, together with hardware
power-present, installs the configurable deadline (default 300ms). MagicPullDown
can install a separate epoch-bound two-second deadline before submitting 0x70.
Transfer failure therefore cannot mean no deadline was installed.

Evidence: 0x5ed7894/0x5ed8a8c (transport jobs), 0x6155f70 (CBTL start),
0x6155700 (RX/CRC completion), 0x5ed71bc (owner cancellation),
0x5ef4ba0/0x5ef4c90 (matching 0x73/0x71 state responses), 0x5ef571c
(MagicPullDown), 0x5ef5b24 (normal handshake). These are behavioral facts from
the same hashed image, not included proprietary instructions or firmware bytes.

The missing production owner must preserve the full route/capability and cable
generation, serialize with existing controller operations, reject late callbacks
after detach, and drain IRQ/timer/owner work before teardown. Initial controller
mode/routing and role/power policy remain unresolved, so no active IDBus
transaction, deadline producer or source readiness is enabled by this patch.

Unactivated CBTL transaction core and bounded Dock event producer
---------------------------------------------------------------
The default-off reference object now also links apple-cbtl-idbus.c. It has no
probe, registration, interrupt, workqueue, timer or board caller; enabling the
Kconfig option alone does not issue IDBus traffic. This is a reusable transport
state machine, NOT yet the actual Tristar hardware owner. The production wrapper
must share the existing Tristar register/policy mutex, cached-capability reads,
mux operations and IRQ processing. It must retain the real source and controller
lifetimes, supply the hardware prepare/recover operations, dispatch timed events,
drain every completion consumer and then integrate the source-event reducer.
Those integrations remain missing. No source snapshot becomes ready here.

The core retains an immutable full six-byte 75 capability payload, both decoded
routes, orientation, validated mode, owner, Dock command state and generation
with each queued job. Its fixed eight-record queue admits one active transaction;
completed records are copied out, not dispatched through unmanaged callback
pointers. Job and cable identities never wrap. A detach invalidates generation
before attempting cancellation; late IRQs and deferred source responses cannot
mutate the new cable. Active cancellation failure retains the active record and
quarantines the controller. Stop cannot claim drain until active cancellation
succeeds and every completed record is consumed. A real wrapper must additionally
join its IRQ, timer, worker and external consumers: these APIs do not do that.

Framing implements 1..64-byte TX/RX, reversed TX, receive count at 60, start at
61, ordinary mode-nibble-zero verification before the separate start write, and
the documented alternate RX count. A timer is armed only after successful start.
Failed starts may have committed and require abort/reset/drain before reuse.
Read/CRC failures never publish response bytes. The hardware recovery callback
must perform both abort and the controller reset/stale-IRQ drain; its success
cannot mean only that the abort register was written. The reset helper's time
unit is not established, so this code does not invent a physical reset delay.
All entry points require the same external serialization. Token checks are NOT
a substitute for draining hardware status before admitting the next job.

The bounded Dock helper accepts only generation/job/command-matched responses.
It records a 73 extended response separately from cached 75 identity; 71 in
states 8/9/10/11 produces the proven pin/power acknowledgement matrix. Cable
invalidation clears response validity without erasing lifecycle-requested pin
state. The numeric accessory-power hook preserves mode != 1 request semantics.
Policy-approved MagicPullDown preparation creates its two-second epoch-bound
deadline and companion event BEFORE transport submission. A failed submission
therefore does not remove that event. Matching successful state 6/71 plus an
explicit validated hardware-power predicate removes the companion and creates
the configurable persistent deadline. Wrong opcode, read/CRC/transport failure,
stale generation or mismatched command does not create it. Effective source
epoch advancement prunes only the older epoch-bound event, not the persistent
handshake deadline. This helper does not implement complete event reduction,
source debounce/publication, command selection, special-open lifecycle or the
power/handshake policy. In particular its result is not either shutdown property.

Additional evidence: source-history timestamp at 5ef218c/5ef2284; accessory
PowerModeOff=1 and request production at 5e7e4d0/5ef6524; unique special-open
client membership at 5ed9860/5ed991c; requested/acknowledged pin separation at
5ef6588/5ef4ef4; three-state handshake property at 5ef2f5c. The same hashed J71b
image named above is the evidence source. These establish producer contracts,
not a current cable's live policy values, and do not justify treating unknown
inputs as disabled or absent. Actual runtime-option providers remain missing.

run.sh now compiles the actual transaction/event core for host testing. Tests
cover all 16,384 TX/RX length, encoding and verification combinations, invalid
lengths and ownership, queue exhaustion, immutable request/cable snapshots,
identifier/time overflow, start/read/verification failure including an error
after committed start, CRC/IRQ errors, timeout-versus-IRQ order, queued-owner
cancellation preserving another owner, failed abort retention, stale generations,
stop/result drain, and the bounded Dock event/response cases. They do not test
Linux mutex/IRQ/workqueue semantics, real Tristar integration or live hardware.

Review corrections: timeout starts from a monotonic sample taken after the
successful start write, so synchronous register verification does not consume
its duration. A successful-completion retire hook must acknowledge/drain old
hardware status before reuse. Owner registration has a separate nonwrapping
epoch: cancellation invalidates queued, completed and already-copied results,
and reopening the same owner identity cannot resurrect them. Consumers must
check result_current under the shared lock immediately before publication;
cable-only current is insufficient. Internal drained does not certify external
work/IRQ/timer consumers have joined. Tests include slow I/O, cancel/reopen after
completion, post-success stale-status drain, failed recovery on start/IRQ/timeout/
retire, and fixed independent CRC vectors. The bounded property-state helper
also preserves unknown/completed/failed results and the distinct inflow-hold
rules; it still does not implement that property's full event notification path.
When canceling a transport owner, its consumer first invalidates the exact Dock
pending token with cbtl_dock_cancel under the shared lock. This retains already
produced events and acknowledgements while allowing a reopened owner to proceed;
it does not require an unrelated full cable reset.
